# Service Layer

## The `api<T>` Wrapper

All service methods use the generic `api<T>` wrapper from `services/api/client.ts` instead of manual try/catch:

```typescript
// ✅ Correct
import { api, apiClient } from './api/client';

export const ordersService = {
  getOrders: (companyId: number) =>
    api<Order[]>(apiClient.get(`/api/v1/companies/${companyId}/orders`)),

  createOrder: (companyId: number, data: CreateOrderPayload) =>
    api<Order>(apiClient.post(`/api/v1/companies/${companyId}/orders`, data)),
};
```

```typescript
// ❌ Wrong — boilerplate try/catch
export const ordersService = {
  getOrders: async (companyId: number) => {
    try {
      const response = await apiClient.get(`/api/v1/companies/${companyId}/orders`);
      return response.data;
    } catch (error) {
      throw handleApiError(error);
    }
  },
};
```

## Rules

- `api<T>` unwraps `AxiosResponse<T>` to `T` and calls `handleApiError` on failure.
- Services are plain objects with methods, not classes.
- One service file per domain (`orders.ts`, `threads.ts`, `auth.ts`).
- Never import `handleApiError` in service files — `api<T>` handles it.
